\title{Reasoning}
\date{2026-01-15}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\transclude{CLT2V}

\transclude{CLT2W}

\p{\strong{Guiding philosophy.} A machine that can reliably solve maths problems of varying difficulty and from various forms of maths can be considered a “reasoning machine”.}

\transclude{CLT2X}

\p{If you are given a collection of rules #{\lbrace P_i \subset Q_i\rbrace } along with a collection of known facts #{\lbrace R_j\rbrace }, then there are two related, yet fundamentally different, ways for you to reason.}

\p{Firstly, say #{X} is some statement that you would like to know whether or not it is true, at least based on your core knowledge. One way to do this is to find #{X} among all consequents (RHS) of your rule set and then look at all associated antecedents (LHS):}

##{X \subset P_n \subset P_{n-1} \subset \cdots}

\p{After recursion you can determine either #{X} is true \em{or} your knowledge is insufficient either way. This form of reasoning is known as \em{backchaining}, a.k.a. \em{goal driven reasoning}.}

\p{On the other hand, if instead one starts by asking “what is true?”, one would look at all antecedents and try to match with the knowledge base #{\lbrace R_j\rbrace }; each time a match is found, add the consequent to the knowledge base. This form of reasoning is called \em{forward-chaining} or \em{data driven reasoning}.}

\p{We will primarily consider building machines that forward chain.}

\transclude{CLT2Y}

\p{We generally express members of the WM as tuples of the form}

##{(\text{type}, \text{attribute}_1 : \text{value}_1, \text{attribute}_2 : \text{value}_2, \ldots, a_n : v_n).}

\transclude{CLT2Z}

\p{Production rule antecedents might look like}

##{A = (\text{type}, \text{attribute}_1 : \text{specification}_1, \ldots, \text{attribute}_n : \text{specification}_n)}

\p{where each specification is one of the following:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{1.} an atom (e.g. number, name, string, …);}
\li{\strong{2.} a variable;}
\li{\strong{3.} an expression, denoted with #{[\; \; ]};}
\li{\strong{4.} a test, denoted with #{\lbrace \; \; \rbrace }.}
}

\transclude{CLT30}

\transclude{CLT35}

\p{Production rule consequents are actions of one of the following three forms:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{1.} #{\text{Add}\; e}: this adds the element #{e} to working memory.}
\li{\strong{2.} #{\text{Remove}\; i}: this removes members of working memory that satisfy the #{i}th antecedent condition. Note this cannot be used if the #{i}th condition is a negative condition.}
\li{\strong{3.} #{\text{Modify}\; i\; (\text{attribute} : \text{specification})}: this modifies members of working memory satisfying condition #{i} by applying the specification to the attribute.}
}

\transclude{CLT36}

\transclude{CLT37}

\transclude{CLT38}

\transclude{CLT39}

\p{\strong{Some things to consider.}}

\ul{
\li{\em{Conflict resolution.} In a large enough production system, you will almost certainly run into situations where a given member of working memory triggers \em{multiple} rules at once. What order should you do them in? Some ideas here include: randomly, provide an order for rule evaluation ahead of time, choose rules based on frequency or recency, etc. There is no “right” choice here. It is largely context dependent.}
\li{\em{Efficiency.} It has been shown that in practice the vast majority of time spent by production systems is in rule matching. Thus there is a method called the \em{Rete method} where one encodes all antecedents into a large network, and uses graph algorithms for efficient traversal. Because the rules of production systems don’t change over time, much of the heavy lifting can be coded ahead of time.}
\li{\em{Explicit use.} Production systems are one of the most successful versions of simple reasoning systems. They appear in the form of \em{expert systems}, \em{MYCIN} (medical diagnosis) and \em{XCON} (computer configuration), which introduced context to allow for parallel processing of rules.}
}

\p{After all this, however, a key question remains: where does it all come from? Certainly designing a production system for counting days in a year is easy, because there are few rules that have no ambiguity.}

\p{The aforementioned MYCIN used a production system for diagnosing bacterial infections. The system had thousands of rules based on expert advice from doctors.}
